  cta={{ href: '/', label: 'Browse all games' }}
>
  <p>
    It's one of the most common — and most reasonable — questions players ask: <em>was that game
    even possible to win?</em> The honest answer is that it depends entirely on which game you're
    playing, and that's worth understanding properly. There are really two different questions
    hiding inside "is this winnable?"
  </p>
  <p>
    The first is: <strong>is this particular deal solvable at all?</strong> That's a fact about
    the shuffle — some arrangements of cards simply cannot be cleared by anyone. The second is:
    <strong>did I play well enough to win the deals that were winnable?</strong> That's about your
    decisions. Most frustration comes from confusing the two — blaming the deal for a misplay, or
    blaming yourself for a deal that never had a solution. Here's the truth, game by game.
  </p>

  <div class="tldr">
    <p class="tldr-title">The short answer</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Guaranteed winnable:</strong> Sudoku (by logic) and Mahjong (by construction) — every deal, always.</li>
      <li><strong>Almost always winnable:</strong> FreeCell and Eight Off — losses are nearly always a misplay, not a bad deal.</li>
      <li><strong>Depends on the shuffle:</strong> Klondike, Spider, Yukon, Scorpion, Forty Thieves, Pyramid, TriPeaks and Golf — skill matters, but some deals are simply impossible.</li>
      <li><strong>Can require a guess:</strong> Minesweeper. And 2048 has no "deal" to be unwinnable — it's pure skill.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Winnability at a glance</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Game</th><th>Is every deal winnable?</th><th>The honest detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th><a href="/sudoku/">Sudoku</a></th><td>Yes — guaranteed</td><td>Unique solution, solvable by pure logic at every difficulty. No guessing, ever.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/mahjong/">Mahjong</a></th><td>Yes — guaranteed</td><td>Built by reverse-construction, so a full solution always exists (you can still strand yourself).</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a></th><td>Almost always</td><td>Only <a href="/freecell/deal/11982/">#11982</a> of the classic 32,000 is unsolvable. A loss is nearly always a misplay.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/eight-off/">Eight Off</a></th><td>Almost always</td><td>Eight free cells make it, like FreeCell, winnable on the vast majority of deals.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/klondike/">Klondike</a></th><td>No — depends</td><td>~80% of Draw 1 deals are theoretically winnable; Draw 3 is harder. Some deals are impossible.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/spider/">Spider</a></th><td>No — depends</td><td>1-suit is forgiving; <a href="/spider-2-suits/">2</a> and <a href="/spider-4-suits/">4 suits</a> much less so. Not pre-screened.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/yukon/">Yukon</a></th><td>No — depends</td><td>All cards face up and no stock, so it's skill-heavy — but a fair share of deals can't be won.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/scorpion/">Scorpion</a></th><td>No — depends</td><td>Build-down-in-suit with buried cards; many deals are winnable, some are not.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/forty-thieves/">Forty Thieves</a></th><td>No — hard</td><td>Two decks, strict building, one card at a time — a low win rate even with good play.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/pyramid/">Pyramid</a>, <a href="/tripeaks/">TriPeaks</a>, <a href="/golf/">Golf</a></th><td>Often not</td><td>Stock-limited, so the cards you need may be unreachable. Good play wins the marginal ones.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/minesweeper/">Minesweeper</a></th><td>Not always</td><td>The first click is always safe, but some boards reach a point where a guess is unavoidable.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><a href="/2048/">2048</a></th><td>N/A — pure skill</td><td>There's no deal to be "unsolvable"; reaching the target tile is down to technique.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Winnability, game by game</h2>

  <h3>Klondike</h3>
  <p>
    Classic <a href="/klondike/">Klondike</a> is part skill, part luck. With perfect play, roughly
    <strong>80% of Draw 1 deals are theoretically winnable</strong> — but real win rates are far
    lower, because perfect play means seeing every consequence in advance, which almost nobody
    does. <a href="/klondike-draw-3/">Draw 3</a> is harder, since only every third stock card is
    initially reachable. And crucially, <strong>some deals are genuinely unwinnable</strong> no
    matter how flawlessly you play. So if you lose a Klondike game, it could be a bad deal — or it
    could be a line you'd take back with hindsight. Our
    <a href="/guides/how-to-win-klondike-solitaire/">how to win Klondike</a> and
    <a href="/guides/klondike-draw-1-vs-draw-3/">Draw 1 vs Draw 3</a> guides cover how to give
    yourself the best chance.
  </p>

  <h3>Spider</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/spider/">Spider</a> winnability depends heavily on how many suits you play with and
    on the choices you make. One-suit deals are forgiving; <a href="/spider-2-suits/">two suits</a>
    and especially <a href="/spider-4-suits/">four suits</a> demand careful sequencing and leave
    far less room for error. Spider deals are <strong>not pre-screened for solvability</strong>, so
    a given hand may or may not be beatable — and even a winnable one can be lost by committing to
    the wrong order of plays early. The <a href="/guides/spider-solitaire-strategy/">Spider
    strategy guide</a> and <a href="/guides/spider-1-2-4-suits/">1, 2 and 4 suits</a> breakdown
    explain how to keep your options open.
  </p>

  <h3>FreeCell</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a> is the standout: <strong>almost every deal is winnable.</strong>
    Because all cards are dealt face-up, there's no hidden information — the puzzle is fully open
    from the start. Of the classic 32,000 numbered Microsoft deals, only <strong>game #11982 has
    been proven unsolvable</strong>. That single exception is famous precisely because it's so rare.
    The practical upshot is liberating: a FreeCell loss is nearly always a <strong>misplay, not a
    bad deal</strong>. That makes it the ideal game for practising clean, deliberate decision-making
    — see the <a href="/guides/freecell-strategy/">FreeCell strategy guide</a> and our
    <a href="/guides/freecell-vs-klondike/">FreeCell vs Klondike comparison</a>. You can also play
    any specific deal by its number on the <a href="/freecell/deals/">FreeCell deal numbers</a> page
    — including the famously unsolvable <a href="/freecell/deal/11982/">#11982</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Eight Off</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/eight-off/">Eight Off</a> is FreeCell's close cousin, and it shares the good news:
    with <strong>eight</strong> free cells instead of four, the vast majority of deals are winnable.
    The extra space gives you more room to manoeuvre, so — as with FreeCell — a loss is much more
    often a planning slip than an impossible deal. The catch is that those eight cells are easy to
    clog; spend them carefully and keep one cascade working toward empty.
  </p>

  <h3>Yukon and Scorpion</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/yukon/">Yukon</a> and <a href="/scorpion/">Scorpion</a> both deal most cards face up
    with no stock to fall back on, which makes them <strong>skill-heavy but not guaranteed</strong>.
    In Yukon you can move any face-up group regardless of order, which opens up tangled positions;
    in Scorpion you build strictly down in suit, so a card buried under the wrong sequence can lock
    a deal up. Deals aren't pre-screened in either game, so a fair share simply can't be won — but
    good order of play rescues many that look hopeless. See the
    <a href="/guides/yukon-solitaire-rules/">Yukon rules</a> and
    <a href="/guides/scorpion-solitaire-rules/">Scorpion rules</a> to get the most out of each.
  </p>

  <h3>Forty Thieves</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/forty-thieves/">Forty Thieves</a> is the serious one. Two full decks, building down
    <em>in suit</em>, and only one card moved at a time mean <strong>many deals are unwinnable</strong>
    and even the winnable ones demand patience — historically it's one of the lowest win-rate
    solitaires. That difficulty is the appeal: a cleared Forty Thieves board is genuinely earned.
    The <a href="/guides/forty-thieves-rules/">Forty Thieves rules</a> explain how to give yourself
    the best chance.
  </p>

  <h3>Pyramid, TriPeaks and Golf</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/pyramid/">Pyramid</a>, <a href="/tripeaks/">TriPeaks</a> and
    <a href="/golf/">Golf</a> are a different breed. They depend heavily on the shuffle and on a
    limited stock, and <strong>many deals simply cannot be cleared</strong> no matter how well you
    play — the cards you need may be buried where you can never reach them in time. That isn't a
    flaw; it's the nature of these games, and a big part of why a fully cleared board feels so
    satisfying. Skill still matters a great deal — good order of play wins many marginal hands —
    but accept that some deals were never going to fall. Our
    <a href="/guides/pyramid-solitaire-strategy/">Pyramid</a>,
    <a href="/guides/tripeaks-solitaire-strategy/">TriPeaks</a> and
    <a href="/guides/golf-solitaire-strategy/">Golf</a> strategy guides help you win as many of
    the winnable ones as possible.
  </p>

  <h3>Sudoku — guaranteed solvable by logic</h3>
  <p>
    Here CardHearth makes a firm promise. <strong>Every CardHearth <a href="/sudoku/">Sudoku</a>
    has a unique solution and can be solved by pure logic, with no guessing required.</strong>
    Whatever the difficulty, there is always a logical next step to find — you'll never reach a
    point where the only option is to flip a coin. If you're stuck, the answer is a technique you
    haven't spotted yet, not bad luck. Learn the methods in our
    <a href="/guides/sudoku-solving-techniques/">solving techniques guide</a> and see how the tiers
    differ in the <a href="/guides/sudoku-difficulty-levels/">difficulty levels guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Mahjong — guaranteed solvable by construction</h3>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/mahjong/">Mahjong</a> games carry a similar guarantee. <strong>Every CardHearth
    Mahjong layout is built by reverse-construction</strong> — the tiles are placed by working
    backward from a fully cleared board, so a complete solution is guaranteed to exist. That said,
    "solvable" is not the same as "automatic": you can still <strong>strand yourself</strong> by
    removing the wrong matching pair and burying a tile you needed. The deal is always beatable;
    winning it is up to your choices. The <a href="/guides/mahjong-solitaire-strategy/">Mahjong
    strategy guide</a> shows how to avoid painting yourself into a corner.
  </p>

  <h3>Minesweeper — sometimes you must guess</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/minesweeper/">Minesweeper</a> is the honest outlier among the puzzles. Your
    <strong>first click is always safe</strong>, and the great majority of a board is solved by pure
    logic — but some positions genuinely reduce to a <strong>50/50 guess</strong>, where two squares
    are equally likely to hide a mine and nothing on the board can tell them apart. That's a known
    property of standard Minesweeper, not a flaw in the deal. The
    <a href="/guides/minesweeper-strategy/">Minesweeper strategy guide</a> shows how to push the
    guessing as late and as rare as possible.
  </p>

  <h3>2048 — no deal to be unwinnable</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/2048/">2048</a> is different in kind: there's no shuffle or deal that could be
    "unsolvable". New tiles appear in random spots, but reaching the 2048 tile (and beyond) is a
    matter of <strong>technique</strong> — keep your biggest tile cornered and build in one
    direction. If you fall short, it's the method to refine, not the luck to blame; the
    <a href="/guides/2048-strategy/">2048 strategy guide</a> has the corner-and-snake approach.
  </p>

  <h2>What CardHearth guarantees</h2>
  <p>
    We think you deserve to know exactly where you stand, so here's what we promise:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sudoku is always solvable by logic.</strong> Unique solution, no guessing, ever —
      at every difficulty.</li>
    <li><strong>Mahjong is always solvable by construction.</strong> Each layout is built backward
      from a cleared board, so a full solution always exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Unlimited undo</strong> on every game. Explore a line, and if it dead-ends, take it
      back and try another — that's the fastest way to learn whether a deal was the problem or your
      plan was.</li>
    <li><strong>Replay the same deal.</strong> Because every deal is numbered, you can run the exact
      same hand again to test a different approach, or share it with a friend. If you beat it on the
      second try with smarter play, you've just proven the deal was winnable all along.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Together, undo and replay are the honest way to settle the "was that winnable?" question for
    games like Klondike and Spider, where deals aren't pre-screened — and for FreeCell, they make
    it almost certain you can find the solution that was there the whole time.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Want a game where every deal is guaranteed beatable? Try <a href="/sudoku/">Sudoku</a> or
    <a href="/mahjong/">Mahjong</a>. Prefer a near-certain solve that rewards careful thought? Play
    <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a>. Or test your skill against the luck of the shuffle in
    <a href="/klondike/">Klondike</a>, <a href="/spider/">Spider</a>,
    <a href="/pyramid/">Pyramid</a>, <a href="/tripeaks/">TriPeaks</a> and
    <a href="/golf/">Golf</a>. Whatever you pick, our <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> has a
    strategy walkthrough to help you win more of the deals that can be won.
  </p>
</Article>
